WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.883
Paula leads the migration of a legacy system, from an old framework to a new one.

00:00:07.183 --> 00:00:08.745
There are four hundred files.

00:00:09.045 --> 00:00:11.457
Some only need their imports swapped.

00:00:11.757 --> 00:00:15.496
Others have logic whose meaning changes in the new framework.

00:00:15.796 --> 00:00:19.311
And some depend on each other, and have to migrate together.

00:00:19.961 --> 00:00:23.728
The first attempt was a single agent walking through the files.

00:00:24.028 --> 00:00:28.807
Halfway through, the context was full, and the early decisions, like how to handle

00:00:28.807 --> 00:00:30.905
the new event system, had been lost.

00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:35.740
The second attempt was a script that sent each file to an isolated call.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.134
Fast.

00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:41.266
But files that depended on each other came out inconsistent.

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:43.482
What works has both parts.

00:00:43.782 --> 00:00:47.301
Someone who looks at the whole, and decides how to split it.

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:50.426
And workers who do each part, with focus.

00:00:50.726 --> 00:00:54.687
The split changes for every module, so it can't be fixed in code.

00:00:54.987 --> 00:00:56.429
That's this lesson.

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:00.093
Separate three roles.

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:06.332
The orchestrator reads the task and its context, and produces a list of subtasks,

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:10.511
each with a self contained instruction, and dependencies.

00:01:10.811 --> 00:01:12.158
It doesn't do the work.

00:01:12.808 --> 00:01:17.647
The workers each receive one subtask, do it in their own context, and return a

00:01:17.647 --> 00:01:19.322
result in an agreed format.

00:01:19.622 --> 00:01:22.991
Subtasks without dependencies run in parallel.

00:01:23.641 --> 00:01:28.869
And the synthesizer combines the results, resolves conflicts, and delivers the final

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:29.367
product.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:33.117
It's often the orchestrator itself, in a second call.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.354
The difference from sectioning is who decides the split.

00:01:37.654 --> 00:01:39.054
There, your code.

00:01:39.354 --> 00:01:42.171
Here, the model, looking at the input.

00:01:42.471 --> 00:01:46.587
If you can write the loop that splits the work before you've seen the input, you

00:01:46.587 --> 00:01:47.977
don't need an orchestrator.

00:01:48.277 --> 00:01:49.935
And know the price up front.

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:53.051
This pattern buys time and coverage with money.

00:01:53.351 --> 00:01:58.534
Teams running it at scale have reported using many times the tokens of a regular

00:01:58.534 --> 00:01:58.858
chat.

00:02:00.058 --> 00:02:04.612
The task arrives, with the material it needs, or access to it.

00:02:05.262 --> 00:02:08.779
The orchestrator analyzes it, and decides the split.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.399
The plan is a structured object.

00:02:11.699 --> 00:02:16.788
Subtasks, each one's instruction, dependencies, and a done criterion.

00:02:17.438 --> 00:02:23.082
Workers run in parallel when there's no dependency, each with its own context and

00:02:23.082 --> 00:02:23.570
budget.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.508
Code validates each result, with a schema, tests or compilation, before moving on.

00:02:31.158 --> 00:02:36.001
The synthesizer puts everything together, and resolves inconsistencies.

00:02:36.651 --> 00:02:41.141
And the result goes out, with the plan and the partial results recorded on the

00:02:41.141 --> 00:02:41.487
trace.

00:02:42.687 --> 00:02:46.708
Paula asks: migrate the payments module to the new framework.

00:02:47.008 --> 00:02:50.273
The orchestrator reads the module and returns a plan.

00:02:50.923 --> 00:02:52.344
Four subtasks.

00:02:52.644 --> 00:02:55.976
Models, and events, with no dependencies.

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:58.419
Then service, which depends on both.

00:02:58.719 --> 00:03:01.167
Then the API, which depends on the service.

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:05.413
Each one with a done criterion, like the models tests pass.

00:03:06.063 --> 00:03:09.151
Code turns those dependencies into waves.

00:03:09.451 --> 00:03:12.540
Wave one runs models and events, in parallel.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.471
Wave two runs the service, which receives both results.

00:03:16.771 --> 00:03:18.476
Wave three runs the API.

00:03:18.776 --> 00:03:22.595
Four workers, three waves, and one synthesis at the end.

00:03:23.245 --> 00:03:25.439
And notice the conventions at the top.

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:29.114
Events use the new event bus, never the old emit.

00:03:29.414 --> 00:03:32.501
Domain errors inherit from one payment error class.

00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:34.335
Every worker gets these.

00:03:35.535 --> 00:03:36.713
Here's one for you.

00:03:37.013 --> 00:03:40.763
During the migration, two workers return code that compiles.

00:03:41.063 --> 00:03:45.818
But one handles errors with exceptions, and the other with return codes.

00:03:46.118 --> 00:03:48.334
The synthesis can't reconcile them.

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:50.163
Where's the cheapest fix?

00:03:53.213 --> 00:03:53.951
In the plan.

00:03:54.251 --> 00:03:58.689
The orchestrator fixes the error convention, and every worker receives it.

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:02.520
Decisions that apply to several parts belong in the plan.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:07.672
When the convention reaches every worker before they start, the parts are compatible

00:04:07.672 --> 00:04:08.770
from the beginning.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.009
A stronger synthesizer that rewrites one side pays to redo work that was born

00:04:15.009 --> 00:04:15.808
incoherent.

00:04:16.108 --> 00:04:18.079
And bigger workers don't help either.

00:04:18.379 --> 00:04:23.126
Each one only sees its own subtask, and can't know what the other one decided.

00:04:23.426 --> 00:04:28.576
The rule of thumb: every decision that more than one worker must make comes from the

00:04:28.576 --> 00:04:30.967
orchestrator, before any worker starts.

00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.143
The split is always the same.

00:04:34.443 --> 00:04:37.081
Per file, per page, per section.

00:04:37.381 --> 00:04:38.598
Then split in code.

00:04:39.248 --> 00:04:43.093
The task fits in one agent, with a clean context.

00:04:43.393 --> 00:04:46.577
An orchestrator adds cost, and points of failure.

00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:51.035
Start with a single loop, and split only when the context fills up.

00:04:51.685 --> 00:04:55.383
The parts depend on each other so much that none can run alone.

00:04:55.683 --> 00:05:01.293
Parallelism disappears, and you pay for the plan and the synthesis without saving

00:05:01.293 --> 00:05:01.916
any time.

00:05:02.566 --> 00:05:06.444
And the workers would be decorative roles, passing text along.

00:05:06.744 --> 00:05:12.137
An analyst, a reviewer and a writer, reading the same context and rewriting the same

00:05:12.137 --> 00:05:13.999
answer, split no work at all.

00:05:14.649 --> 00:05:15.877
Quick case.

00:05:16.177 --> 00:05:19.674
Your team wants a weekly summary of thirty store reports.

00:05:19.974 --> 00:05:25.485
Each report becomes a paragraph, and a consolidated version comes out at the end.

00:05:25.785 --> 00:05:28.054
Someone proposes an orchestrator.

00:05:28.354 --> 00:05:29.572
What do you say?

00:05:29.872 --> 00:05:32.692
The split is fixed, one report per part.

00:05:32.992 --> 00:05:34.406
That's a loop in code.

00:05:34.706 --> 00:05:39.361
You get parallelism and a clean context per report, without the plan call, and

00:05:39.361 --> 00:05:41.211
without the risk of a bad plan.

00:05:41.861 --> 00:05:43.310
And a close cousin.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:46.419
Plan then execute also starts with a plan.

00:05:46.719 --> 00:05:48.795
The difference is who executes.

00:05:49.095 --> 00:05:53.886
There, a single agent follows the plan step by step, in the same context.

00:05:54.186 --> 00:06:00.244
Here, each subtask goes to a worker with its own context, and independent ones run

00:06:00.244 --> 00:06:01.500
at the same time.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.953
Use the first when the steps are sequential and need what came before.

00:06:06.253 --> 00:06:11.220
Use this one when the parts fit in separate contexts, and the volume justifies

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:11.984
parallelism.

00:06:13.184 --> 00:06:16.039
Let's put a price on it, with illustrative numbers.

00:06:16.339 --> 00:06:21.760
The plan costs about six thousand tokens, each worker twenty thousand, and the

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.636
synthesis fifteen thousand.

00:06:23.936 --> 00:06:28.723
With four subtasks, the task spends about one hundred one thousand tokens.

00:06:29.023 --> 00:06:33.796
If the orchestrator decides on twenty subtasks, the total goes past four hundred

00:06:33.796 --> 00:06:34.751
twenty thousand.

00:06:35.051 --> 00:06:37.577
And quality doesn't necessarily rise.

00:06:37.877 --> 00:06:42.321
That's why the subtask limit lives in the prompt, and also in code.

00:06:42.621 --> 00:06:44.133
The model picks the split.

00:06:44.433 --> 00:06:46.609
Code decides how much it may cost.

00:06:47.259 --> 00:06:48.569
The failure modes.

00:06:48.869 --> 00:06:53.524
A bad plan, with overlapping subtasks, or one part missing.

00:06:53.824 --> 00:06:58.275
That spoils everything after it, so evaluate the plan on its own.

00:06:58.575 --> 00:07:03.463
For twenty real tasks, write down by hand the parts a good plan must cover.

00:07:03.763 --> 00:07:06.184
A missing part is a coverage failure.

00:07:06.484 --> 00:07:09.672
Two subtasks touching the same file is overlap.

00:07:09.972 --> 00:07:11.971
And validate the plan in code too.

00:07:12.271 --> 00:07:15.496
No cycles, no dependencies pointing nowhere.

00:07:16.146 --> 00:07:17.599
Over-splitting.

00:07:17.899 --> 00:07:23.091
Dozens of trivial subtasks, with very short outputs and a high total cost.

00:07:23.741 --> 00:07:25.466
And incoherence.

00:07:25.766 --> 00:07:29.300
Workers making different decisions about the same problem.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:30.888
You just saw the fix.

00:07:31.188 --> 00:07:33.285
Put conventions in the plan.

00:07:33.935 --> 00:07:39.219
In production, record the plan as a span, with a child span for each worker.

00:07:39.519 --> 00:07:44.643
Put limits on subtasks and waves per task, with an alert when they hit.

00:07:44.943 --> 00:07:49.074
And keep an eye on how often each type of subtask has to be retried.

00:07:49.374 --> 00:07:54.434
A subtask type that always fails is asking for a better worker prompt.

00:07:55.634 --> 00:07:57.826
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:58.126 --> 00:08:01.916
Use an orchestrator only when the split depends on the content.

00:08:02.216 --> 00:08:07.408
Let it write a plan with self contained instructions, dependencies, done criteria

00:08:07.408 --> 00:08:08.882
and shared conventions.

00:08:09.182 --> 00:08:14.813
Let code run the waves, validate each result, and cap how far the split can go.

00:08:15.463 --> 00:08:21.217
In the next lesson, we close the agents track with a real system that uses almost

00:08:21.217 --> 00:08:23.988
everything you've seen: a coding agent.

00:08:24.288 --> 00:08:25.044
See you there.
